   "title": "US Justice Dept sues to block possible Alaska election of Democrat to Senate",
   "url": "https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/world/20261024/us-justice-dept-sues-to-block-possible-alaska-election-of-democrat-to-senate",
   "domain": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "source": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "published": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 02:22:03 GMT",
   "_dt": 1792808523.0,
   "ageDays": 0.03,
   "summary": "The US Justice Department on Thursday sued to preemptively block Alaska from certifying the results of its US Senate race if the Republican incumbent candidate loses by a narrow margin, saying voters could be confused because two Republicans on the ballot have almost identical names. Control of the US Senate could come down to Alaska, where incumbent Republican Senator Dan S. Election law experts said the Justice Department's lawsuit has no merit and seeks relief not allowed under federal law.",
   "summary_zh": "美国司法部周四提起诉讼，试图预先阻止阿拉斯加州认证其美国参议院选举结果，以防共和党现任候选人以微弱差距败选。司法部称，选票上有两名名字几乎相同的共和党人，可能令选民混淆。美国参议院的控制权可能取决于阿拉斯加州，现任共和党参议员丹·S.的选举结果尚未确定。选举法专家表示，美国司法部的诉讼毫无依据，所请求的救济也不符合联邦法律的规定。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The US Justice Department on Thursday sued to preemptively block Alaska from certifying the results of its US Senate race if the Republican incumbent candidate loses by a narrow margin, saying voters could be confused because two Republicans on the ballot have almost identical names. The lawsuit signals the willingness of President Donald Trump's administration to contest the results of the November 3 midterm elections that will decide control of the US Congress. Trump's administration has taken an increasingly aggressive approach to election oversight, pressing states for voter-registration data and arguing that stricter measures are needed to ensure public confidence in the vote. Critics, including both Democratic and some Republican election officials, view the moves as federal overreach. Control of the US Senate could come down to Alaska, where incumbent Republican Senator Dan S. Sullivan is running a closely contested race against Democrat Mary Peltola. The Justice Department's lawsuit, filed in federal court in Anchorage, takes issue with the presence of a third contender in the race, another Republican named Dan J. Sullivan Jr, alleging he is running \"with the intention of misleading supporters of Senator Sullivan into voting for him.\" The lawsuit asks a federal judge to block the certification of an outcome \"that may be impacted by voter confusion,\" so that there can be \"an investigation to determine whether votes cast for Daniel J. Sullivan were meant to be cast for Senator Sullivan.\" Trump told reporters on Friday that he supported the lawsuit, saying \"I'm the one that said do it.\" Election officials around the country have told Reuters they are preparing for potential federal interference in November’s elections, including legal challenges and disputes over voting that could disrupt certification of the results. Trump has a long history of challenging election outcomes and encouraging supporters to contest results he views as unfavorable. Election law experts said the Justice Department's lawsuit has no merit and seeks relief not allowed under federal law. The unelected Sullivan, a former US Forest Service worker and elementary school teacher, filed to run as a candidate on May 29 seeking to unseat Senator Dan S. Sullivan and \"put Alaska first,\" according to his campaign website, which advertised \"Dan Sullivan Challenges Dan Sullivan for US Senate Seat.\" Dan J. Sullivan has denied in interviews with US news outlets that his aim is to cause confusion or divert votes from Dan S. Sullivan. The Alaska Supreme Court in June permitted the unelected Sullivan to appear on the ballot. He will appear without a party designation next to his name, while the senator will be listed as the Republican incumbent. Trump's approval rating has fallen to a record low, hit by rising diesel and gasoline prices and a conflict in the Middle East that has proven deeply unpopular with voters. Public opinion polls suggest Republicans may lose control of one or both houses of Congress, which would likely limit Trump's ability to enact his policies in the final two years of his presidency",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "af"
   ],
