   "summary_zh": "订阅即可解锁这些内容。2026年10月16日，国大党主席兼印度联邦院反对党领袖 Mallikarjun Kharge 致信总理 Narendra Modi，要求在政府划界方案最终确定并提交议会之前召开全体政党会议，讨论这些方案。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription On October 16, 2026, Congress President and Leader of the Opposition (LoP) in the Rajya Sabha, Mallikarjun Kharge, wrote to Prime Minister Narendra Modi, seeking an all-party meeting to discuss the government’s delimitation proposals before they were finalised and introduced in Parliament. It was Mr. Kharge’s fourth letter since April seeking such a meeting. On October 19, Parliamentary Affairs Minister Kiren Rijiju replied, saying that the government had repeatedly sought the Congress’s cooperation, including through letters to Mr. Kharge in March and April and discussions with him and Leader of the Opposition in the Lok Sabha, Rahul Gandhi. Mr. Rijiju also said that the government had held consultations with several other Opposition parties. Yet, his reply showed once again that the Centre had no intention of disclosing its precise proposals on delimitation. Without explicitly saying so, Mr. Rijiju’s letter suggested that the government planned to bring in legislation on delimitation while withholding the details, thereby preventing a possible public debate on the issue. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
