   "title": "ACF Demands Full Disclosure On Nigeria-US Mineral Deal",
   "url": "https://leadership.ng/acf-demands-full-disclosure-on-nigeria-us-mineral-deal/",
   "domain": "leadership.ng",
   "source": "Leadership（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Thu, 22 Oct 2026 07:24:32 +0000",
   "_dt": 1792653872.0,
   "ageDays": 1.82,
   "summary": "ACF warned that the deal could expose Nigeria’s mineral wealth and vulnerable communities to exploitation if adequate safeguards are not in place. The northern socio-cultural organisation said the Federal Government’s alleged reluctance to make the agreement public had raised serious questions about its financial implications, the rights granted to foreign interests, environmental safeguards and the benefits Nigeria and mineral-producing communities stand to gain.",
   "summary_zh": "ACF警告称，如果没有采取充分保障措施，这项协议可能使尼日利亚的矿产财富和脆弱社区遭受剥削。这个北部地区的社会文化组织表示，联邦政府据称不愿公开协议，引发了对若干重大问题的疑问，包括协议对财政的影响、授予外国利益方的权利、环境保护措施，以及尼日利亚和矿产生产社区将从中获得何种利益。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "ACF warned that the deal could expose Nigeria’s mineral wealth and vulnerable communities to exploitation if adequate safeguards are not in place. The northern socio-cultural organisation said the Federal Government’s alleged reluctance to make the agreement public had raised serious questions about its financial implications, the rights granted to foreign interests, environmental safeguards and the benefits Nigeria and mineral-producing communities stand to gain. ACF National Publicity Secretary, Prof. T. A. Muhawwmmad-Baba, in a statement said the Forum supported responsible foreign investment but would not accept an arrangement whose terms and benefits remained opaque. The agreement was reportedly signed in New York on September 23, 2026, in connection with the exploitation of critical and strategic minerals. The Forum insisted that Nigerians deserved to know what the country had committed to the arrangement and what the United States would deliver in return, including investments, financing, infrastructure, technology transfer and industrial development. ACF identified gold, lithium, lead-zinc and other commercially valuable minerals in Northern Nigeria, saying their responsible development could generate employment, increase public revenue, stimulate industrialisation and diversify the regional economy. However, it stressed that the central question was whether the reported deal would translate into measurable benefits for Nigerians or primarily facilitate the extraction and export of raw materials. “The ACF’s concern is not, per se, cooperation with the United States but whether Nigeria, and particularly mineral-producing communities, will receive a fair, transparent and enforceable share of the benefits. “ACF is particularly concerned that the spate of insecurity pervading many parts of the northern region may expose mineral-bearing communities to unlawful exploitation, population displacement, environmental degradation and the loss of legitimate economic opportunities. “The Forum supports responsible foreign investment, but not investment without transparency; mineral development, but not extraction without value addition; and international partnership, but not arrangements whose terms and benefits remain opaque. “Who bears the environmental and security costs? Are there safeguards and other guarantees to protect the environment, post-exploitation? Will there be commitments to firm and enforceable environmental remediation? “Nigeria in general, and the northern region in particular, cannot afford an extractive arrangement in which its resources leave the ground while its people remain poor, unemployed and exposed to environmental damage,” the statement read in part. Expressing particular concern over insecurity across parts of Northern Nigeria, the ACF warned that mineral-bearing communities already threatened by armed groups could face further exploitation, displacement and the loss of legitimate economic opportunities. It said the Federal Government and relevant security and regulatory agencies must explain how they intended to prevent illegal mining, protect mining sites and host communities, and ensure that proceeds from lawful mineral development supported legitimate economic activities. We’ve got the edge. Get real-time reports, breaking scoops, and exclusive angles delivered straight to your phone. Don’t settle for stale news. Join LEADERSHIP NEWS on WhatsApp for 24/7 updates →",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "ng"
   ],
   "topic": [
    "min",
    "eco"
   ],
   "tier": 2,
   "actor": "美国",
   "alsoUS": false,
   "cnHit": false,
   "usHit": true,
